# elohim-protocol/ — the protocol canon (governed)

This directory is the vision. Its governance base is a **sibling pair of ontology guards** (bound from
`.claude/epr-meta/policies.yaml`, both at `@5`): a write that NET-NEW introduces apex-assertion framing
dispatches a bounded storyteller review against the legitimate uses and settled canon without stopping
the active authoring pass.

- **`sovereignty-ontology-guard@5`** — "self-sovereign" as the top identity tier, "true data
  sovereignty" as a goal. Three frames: *adversary · bounded · bridge-legibility*.
- **`ownership-ontology-guard@5`** — ownership as the right relationship to a resource. Four frames:
  *adversary · bounded · external-legibility · **inalienable***.

They are a **pair, not two rules**: ownership says *"mine, not yours"*, sovereignty says *"me, not
us"* — one refusal of relationship in two grammars, so each cross-references the other and whoever
learns one lesson inherits the other. The **inalienable** frame exists because the other three did not
fit a person's absolute claim over their own body, likeness, or intimate images, and that gap made the
guard teach the wrong lesson on the cases that matter most: an author writing correctly about a
survivor's claim was being nudged to reframe toward custody, weakening it.

Both are **guards, not bans** — a storyteller sidecar reports alignment or a bounded reframe while
authoring continues — and both fire only on *introduction*, so editing a doc to *clean* its framing
is never trapped. Declaring
`sovereignty-frame:` or `stewardship-frame:` in a doc quiets them (and the PostToolUse logger) where
the frame is already adjudicated.

Each carries a **four-rung progressive-disclosure chain** in its lineage — *review → architecture → law
→ warrant* — so an agent can stop at the rung that answers its question rather than being handed a
rule to memorize. The chain is deliberately links, not prose: a guard that grew to contain its own
warrant would be the cage it warns against (`confession.md` — the protocol "never deceives the agent
that the cage is liberty").

Dismissals aggregate through `sovereignty-guard-signal.py` (PostToolUse) into a drift ledger so the
rule itself surfaces for evaluation: the protocol exercising its own governance over the bytes of this
repo. (The ownership guard has no equivalent sink yet — a known asymmetry, not a decision.)

## Absolutes guard — a promise names its enforcer

**`absolutes-guard@1`** is the same machinery pointed at a different defect: an *absolute* on a trust
surface (this canon, every `genesis/docs/**` and `genesis/a2o/features/**` story, `app/**/*.html`,
`app/elohim-elements/**`, the content chrome's `omni-element.js`). *Always, never, forever, guaranteed,
impossible, every time, at all times, no one can, everyone, 100%, without exception* — the protocol is
graduated, governed, revocable and plural (reach narrows, gates re-admit, a steward acts under a
StewardshipGrant for a person who holds their rights through others, trust lags delivery), so prose that
says one of these usually promises more than the system keeps. The lexicon is the frame atom
`elohim/sdk/schemas/v1/frames/frame-absolute-promise.json`; the scan is `.claude/scripts/_lib/absolutes.py`
(word-bounded, prose only, net-new only).

Two honest repairs, nothing else:

- **Cite the enforcer** in the same sentence or line, and it must resolve — `(enforced: <repo path |
  policy:<id>@<version> | seam:<id>>)` in prose (a path may carry `#anchor`, `:line` or `::test`), or
  `// absolute: <ref>` / `<!-- absolute: <ref> -->` on or just above a UI string. A test, a refusing
  predicate, a registered policy or a schema constraint is an enforcer; a sentence that says so is not.
  A negated capability ("no one can read it") needs the citation too.
- **Say what actually holds** — *by default*, *unless …*, *until …*, *as far as this device knows*.

Quoted speech, blockquotes and declared testimony (`testimony: true`, the ontology atom's paths) are
exempt; code comments and identifiers are never scanned. Like the pair above it is a guard, not a ban: a
`dispatch` storyteller review, and `absolutes-guard-signal.py` (PostToolUse) lists each net-new absolute
with its line, logs it to `.claude/data/absolutes-guard.jsonl` and folds it onto `absolutes-landings@1`
(ceiling `absolutes-landings-ceiling@1`, hard 5) so the rule itself comes up for review.

## Manifesto-epic blind-reader review

Every `epic*.md` authoring pass routes to the general `blind-reader` using the `manifesto-epic`
profile. The test is deliberately harsher than ordinary editorial review: the reader receives no
manifesto, sibling epic, glossary, implementation history, or author explanation. It must be able to
recover the human condition being answered, the future the epic makes imaginable, who gains agency,
why that change is valuable, and how the sections form one coherent emotional and ethical movement.
A large findings set is useful; revisions go to a new blind reader until the epic stands alone.
